course: french
lesson: 29
title: Avoir: to have
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Avoir means "to have": j'ai (I have), tu as, il a and elle a, on a, nous avons, vous avez, ils ont and elles ont - another verb you learn by heart, a sneeze and a hiccup: "zhay, tu A, ee-LA".
explain: French uses avoir for lots of things English says with "to be": you have hunger (j'ai faim), thirst (j'ai soif), fear (j'ai peur) and years (j'ai dix ans).
explain: Picture a holiday cottage in France at night: a huge spider runs across the floor, you jump on the bed and shout "J'ai peur !" - and your French friend laughs, "Il y a une araignée !", there's a spider!
explain: Il y a means "there is" or "there are", for one thing or for many: il y a un chat, il y a trois chats.
explain: Be careful with two pairs: ils ont (they have, with a buzzing z) and ils sont (they are, with a hissing s); and a with no accent means "has", while à with an accent means "to" or "at".
fact: Avoir (to have): j'ai, tu as, il a, elle a, on a, nous avons, vous avez, ils ont, elles ont; it must be learned by heart.
fact: Ils ont (they have) sounds "eel-ZON"; ils sont (they are) sounds "eel SON".
fact: Je becomes j' before a vowel: j'ai, j'aime, j'habite; never "je ai".
fact: J'ai peur means "I'm scared"; French says "I have fear".
fact: J'ai faim means "I'm hungry" and j'ai soif means "I'm thirsty".
fact: Tu as un animal ? means "Do you have a pet?"; a good answer is "Oui, j'ai un poisson rouge" (a goldfish).
fact: Il y a means "there is" or "there are": il y a un chat sur le lit, il y a trois chats.
fact: Nous verbs usually end in -ons and vous verbs in -ez: nous avons, vous avez.
fact: Hair and eyes are described with avoir: elle a les cheveux longs, il a les yeux verts.
fact: A with no accent comes from avoir and means "has"; à with an accent means "to" or "at": il a un chien, il va à Paris.
fact: J'ai mal à la tête means "I have a headache"; j'ai mal à + body part says where it hurts.
exercise: Which is the right order of avoir? | j'ai, tu as, il a | je ai, tu as, il a | j'ai, tu a, il as | j'ai, tu es, il est
exercise: Jayden writes "Je ai un chien." What is right? | J'ai un chien | Je a un chien | Je ai un chien | J'est un chien
exercise: Ruby sees a spider. What does she say? | J'ai peur ! | Je suis peur ! | J'ai très faim ! | Je fais peur !
exercise: Noah wants water after a match. What does he say? | J'ai soif | J'ai très faim | Je suis soif | J'ai froid
exercise: Amira means "they have two cats". What is right? | Ils ont deux chats | Ils sont deux chats | Ils a deux chats | Ils avons deux chats
exercise: How does ils ont sound? | "eel-ZON", with a z | "eel SON", with an s | "eel ONT", with a t | "eel-TON", with a t
exercise: Sam is asked "Tu as un animal ?" What is a good answer? | Oui, j'ai un poisson rouge | Oui, je suis un poisson rouge | Oui, il y a un poisson | Oui, tu as un poisson rouge
exercise: What does "Il y a un chat sur le lit" mean? | There is a cat on the bed | He has a cat on the bed | The cat is in the bed | It is a cat on a bed
exercise: Can il y a be used for many things? | Yes: il y a trois chats | No: you need il y ont | No: you need ils y a | Yes: but only for people
exercise: Harvey writes "Nous avez un chien." What is right? | Nous avons un chien | Nous avez un chien | Nous ont un chien | Nous as un chien
exercise: Which is right for "you have" to a group? | vous avez | vous avons | vous avoir | vous ont
exercise: Ava's friend has long hair. Which is right? | Elle a les cheveux longs | Elle est les cheveux longs | Elle a les cheveux long | Elle à les cheveux longs
exercise: Which sentence uses a correctly? | Il a un chat | Il va a Paris | Il à un petit chat | Il a à un chat
exercise: Ollie has a headache. What does he say? | J'ai mal à la tête | Je suis mal à la tête | J'ai la tête mal | J'ai mal la tête
exercise: Which pair means "we have" and "they have"? | nous avons, ils ont | nous avez, ils sont | nous sommes, ils ont | nous avons, ils sont
test: Charlotte's mate says, "J'ai, tu as, il a - it's like a sneeze then a hiccup!" Which one comes next for elle? | elle a | elle as | elle est | elle ai
test: Your cousin in London goes, "Je ai a chien, bare short innit." Fix the whole thing. | J'ai un chien | Je ai un chien | J'ai a chien | Je a un chien
test: What would happen if you said "Ils sont deux chats" to a French friend? | They'd hear "they are two cats", not "they have" | They'd hear "they have two cats" perfectly | They'd think the cats were asleep | They'd think you were counting to two
test: A cartoon dog says "Je suis soif" and pants. What should the bubble say? | J'ai soif | Je suis faim | Moi, je soif | J'ai soifs
test: Lily from Ottawa says, "Il y a means there is, eh." Her brother says it only works for one thing. Who's right? | Lily: il y a works for one or many | Her brother: use ils y a for many | Her brother: use il y ont for many | Neither: il y a means he has
test: Harvey from Glasgow goes, "Och, the endings are doin' ma heid in!" (doing my head in = driving me mad) Which pair of endings helps him? | nous ends -ons, vous ends -ez | nous ends -ez, vous ends -ons | nous ends -ent, vous ends -er | nous ends -ont, vous ends -as
test: Ethan from Boston writes "Mon frère à un chien." He says, "Wicked confusing, one tiny line." What's the fix? | a with no accent, because it means has | à is right, because it means has | as with an s, because it is il | est, because the dog is his
test: ★★★ A French poem says: "Il y a un chat. Il a les yeux verts. Il a peur du chien." Which is true? | There is a green-eyed cat that is scared of the dog | There is a dog with green eyes that is scared of a cat | The cat is green and the dog is scared of it | There are three cats and the dog has green eyes
test: ★★★ Fill both gaps: "Nous ___ un chien et ils ___ deux chats." | avons, ont | avez, sont | avons, sont | sommes, ont
test: Ollie from Sydney tells his penpal "Je suis mal à la tête." She goes, "Hein ?" (eh?) What should he have said? | J'ai mal à la tête | Je suis la tête mal | J'ai tête mal | Je mal à la tête
test: Gran says "J'ai peur" just means "I have a pear", my love. What's true? | It means "I'm scared": peur is fear | She's right, peur means pear | It means "I'm hungry" | It means "I have a pair"
exam: Identify the form of avoir which corresponds to the pronoun "ils". | ont | sont | a | avez
exam: Select the expression which correctly conveys, in French, the condition of being thirsty. | J'ai soif | Je suis soif | J'ai faim | Je soif
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "il y a"? | It signifies "there is" or "there are", irrespective of number | It signifies "he has", applicable to persons only | It signifies "there is", applicable to a single item only | It signifies "it is", applicable to the weather only
exam: Determine the form employed to describe hair, in the statement "she has long hair". | Elle a les cheveux longs | Elle est les cheveux longs | Elle à les cheveux longs | Elle a les cheveux long
